(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609010500-x402-client-sdks-hold-no-key")
  (:adr/title "x402 client SDKs hold no key: the signer is injected")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-01")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609010300-a-discovery-surface-is-a-promise-and-nothing-was-checking-it"
                "adr-2609010400-the-fleet-cannot-mine-testnet-gas-and-does-not-need-to"))
  (:adr/body "2026-09-01. x402 client libraries for TypeScript and Python, at
kotoba-lang/x402-sdk, with the signer injected.

## Why an SDK at all

x402 is four HTTP steps: ask, read the 402, pay, ask again with the proof. It
is small enough that a library's job is not to hide it. What it is for is the
handful of details that are easy to get wrong and expensive when they are.

## The four the SDK exists for

**The EIP-712 domain comes from the offer.** Base mainnet USDC declares
`USD Coin`; Base Sepolia USDC declares `USDC`. Signing under the wrong one
produces a signature that recovers a different address, and the payment is
rejected as a bad signature -- which reads like the buyer's fault. The client
refuses an offer carrying no domain rather than guessing one, and raises on an
unknown network rather than defaulting its chain id.

**The cheapest allowed offer wins, never accepts[0].** Sellers list the same
resource on mainnet and on a testnet in their own order. Taking the first is
how a buyer pays real money to rehearse.

**A policy is required.** No policy refuses with `no-policy` rather than
paying. A default ceiling is a limit nobody chose.

**Every failing check is named, not just the first.** An offer on the wrong
network usually names that network's token too, so a caller told only
`network-not-allowed` fixes the network and is refused again.

## The signer is injected

These packages hold no key, sign nothing, and have no cryptography dependency.
The wallet stays wherever its owner put it: viem, ethers, eth-account, a
hardware device, a remote signer. A payment library that wants a key is a
place a key can leak from, and this is the same injection seam the rest of
this workspace uses -- webauthn's verifier, base-l2's transport, kotobase's
sign-fn.

It also keeps the packages honest about scope. They implement `exact`, where
the facilitator submits and pays the gas so a buyer needs no ETH. An offer
asking for `transaction` is refused by name rather than half-supported.

## Two defects the tests did not find

Both came from running against the live facilitator instead of against the
suite.

urllib's default User-Agent gets 403 from Cloudflare, so a Python client using
it cannot reach x402.nexus at all -- and the failure arrives looking like
authorisation rather than like a bot rule. The default fetch now sends a
User-Agent.

`select_offer` handed a None challenge raised AttributeError, reporting a
protocol outcome as a bug in the caller's own code. It refuses with
`not-a-challenge`.

## Languages

Four: TypeScript, Python, Go and Rust, following the layout of
net-kotobase/sdk (packages/<language>, native tooling per package, one wire
contract in spec/wire.md).

Rust injects the HTTP transport as well as the signer, which is why its
dependency list is serde and nothing else: the crate opens no socket, so a
program that already has an HTTP client does not acquire a second one.

The wire contract is transcribed from kotoba-lang/pay's pay.x402 -- what
x402.nexus actually runs -- rather than from a specification the server might
have drifted from.

## Published, and verified from the registry rather than from the build

    npm        @com-kotobalabs/x402@0.1.0
    PyPI       com-kotobalabs-x402@0.1.0
    crates.io  com-kotobalabs-x402@0.1.0
    Go         github.com/kotoba-lang/x402-sdk/packages/go@v0.1.0

Each was installed from its own registry into an empty project and run against
the live facilitator, not merely uploaded. All four printed the same thing:
base-sepolia, 1000 units, domain USDC/2, chainId 84532. A publish that exits 0
is a claim about an upload; an install that runs is a claim about the package.

## The names, and why they are not the obvious ones

Checked before publishing rather than after a rejected upload: x402-sdk and
x402 are taken on crates.io, x402-client on npm and PyPI. This workspace's SDK
is not the first x402 client.

com-kotobalabs is the reverse-DNS form this workspace uses for organisations.
npm has scopes so it carries it as one; PyPI and crates.io have none, so they
carry it as a prefix.

## Ownership is personal for now, and the registries disagree about what that means

    npm org com-kotobalabs   exists, jun784 is owner, the package is in it
    PyPI org com-kotobalabs  does not exist
    GitHub org com-kotobalabs does not exist
    crates.io owner          com-junkawasaki, a personal account

Accepted deliberately (owner, 2026-09-01). Creating a PyPI organisation or a
GitHub organisation is account creation, which is not something this agent
does; both need the owner in a browser, and PyPI's also needs an application
approved by its staff.

So the identity is real on npm and is only a NAME on PyPI and crates.io. That
difference is not cosmetic and is recorded rather than smoothed over: a reader
who assumes org ownership on all four would be wrong on three. Once those orgs
exist, `cargo owner --add github:com-kotobalabs:<team>` grants the crate to a
team without creating anything, and PyPI's project can be transferred.

## Three lessons about credentials, all of them measured

The tokens arrived pasted into chat. That publishes them into a stored
transcript, so all three were flagged for rotation the moment they were used.
The pattern that avoids it is the one the rest of this workspace uses: the
value lives in kagi or 1Password, a tool reads exactly one item by a known
identifier, and it never enters the agent's context. 1Password could not do
that here -- the desktop app's CLI integration is off, so op signin, op read
and op item get all fail identically.

A credentials file is not a credential. ~/.cargo/credentials.toml was present,
and this was reported as \"cargo is already authenticated\". It was not: the
publish returned 403. Only the attempt distinguished them, and the report had
to be corrected.

An upload that succeeds is not a package that resolves. npm returned 404 for
about ten minutes after a publish that exited 0 and printed the version. The
discriminating measurement was an authenticated install, which returned `added
1 package` -- so the package existed and the public read path was lagging,
which is a different thing from a failed publish and needs a different
response. It resolved on its own.

## What is still not published

Nothing. PyPI was the last one.")))
